{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Elohim judged me.</b> He is righteous in His judgment that He did not grant me pregnancy." "<b>And also He heard my voice.</b> And despite this, He accepted my prayer." "<b>And He gave me a son.</b> A staff for my hand and a sign for burial (Yevamot 65a), since he was born upon my knees. Now the mistresses agreed in these words of theirs that the maidservants should be freed — this, in their saying that the sons of the maidservants should be to them as sons, not as slaves, as the law would have been if their mothers were maidservants, as it says: הָאִשָּׁה וִילָדֶיהָ תִּהְיֶה לַאדוֹנֶיהָ (Exodus 21:4). Therefore they did not enslave them as Sarah enslaved Hagar with her husband's consent, as it says: הִנֵּה שִׁפְחָתֵךְ בְּיָדֵךְ (Genesis 16:6). And so all of them were sons of Jacob to inherit him, and all were accepted for remembrance before HaShem on the ephod and the breastplate, since each one of them was reckoned to Jacob as his seed traced after him. The opposite was the case with Ishmael, as it says: כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע (Genesis 21:12)."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:32:29.267984384Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}